<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Go=
od morning! I'm Michael, Principal Engineer here at NAB's Digital Banking P=
latform team. I've been with NAB for about 12 years, leading our frontend a=
rchitecture and modernization efforts. Thanks for taking the time to interv=
iew with us today. Could you start by introducing yourself and telling me a=
bout your experience with asynchronous JavaScript and promise management?</=
p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Good=
 morning Michael! Thank you for having me. I'm excited about this opportuni=
ty at NAB. I'm a Senior Frontend Engineer with 7 years of experience, with =
the last 5 years heavily focused on building large-scale React applications=
.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Currently, I'm the technical lea=
d for a team of 8 engineers at a fintech company where we serve over 3 mill=
ion users. Our platform processes thousands of concurrent financial transac=
tions, which has given me deep experience with <strong>complex asynchronous=
 operations and promise management</strong>.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">What's particularly relevant to =
NAB is that we deal with <strong>real-time payment processing, account sync=
hronization, and multi-step financial workflows</strong> that require sophi=
sticated async coordination. We've implemented everything from <strong>Prom=
ise pooling for rate limiting</strong>, <strong>circuit breakers for extern=
al API calls</strong>, to <strong>custom cancellation patterns for long-run=
ning operations</strong> like document processing and fraud detection.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">I've also contributed to open-so=
urce async utilities and have experience with <strong>AbortController</stro=
ng>, <strong>Web Workers for background processing</strong>, and <strong>Se=
rvice Workers for offline payment capabilities</strong> - all critical for =
banking applications where reliability and user experience are paramount.</=
p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's excellent! The real-time payment processing experience is definitely v=
aluable here. At NAB, we handle similar challenges - millions of customers =
performing transactions, checking balances, and managing their finances sim=
ultaneously. Before we dive into today's coding challenge, I'd love to unde=
rstand your approach to handling promise cancellation. In your current role=
, what scenarios have you encountered where promise cancellation was critic=
al, and how did you solve those challenges?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Grea=
t question! Promise cancellation has been absolutely critical in our fintec=
h environment, and I've encountered several key scenarios:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>1. User-initiated cancel=
lations:</strong>
When users navigate away from a payment flow mid-process, we need to <stron=
g>gracefully cancel pending API calls</strong> to prevent orphaned transact=
ions. We implemented a <strong>route-based cancellation system</strong> usi=
ng AbortController that automatically cancels in-flight requests when users=
 leave a page.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>2. Timeout-based cancell=
ations:</strong>
For regulatory compliance, certain operations like <strong>international tr=
ansfers have strict timeout windows</strong>. We built a <strong>hierarchic=
al timeout system</strong> where different operations have different cancel=
lation thresholds - account verification (30s), fraud checks (60s), regulat=
ory validation (120s).</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>3. Resource optimization=
:</strong>
In our mobile app, we had a <strong>battery drain issue</strong> from backg=
round API polling. We implemented <strong>intelligent cancellation patterns=
</strong> that stop unnecessary requests when the app goes into background =
or when network conditions degrade.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>4. Race condition preven=
tion:</strong>
The most complex scenario was handling <strong>concurrent balance updates</=
strong>. When multiple transactions happen simultaneously, we use <strong>p=
romise cancellation with conflict resolution</strong> - if a newer transact=
ion invalidates an older one, we cancel the older promise and reconcile the=
 state.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Here's a pattern we deve=
loped for financial operations:</strong></p>
